       unsafe { ceesafe_atomic_add_ord_u32(this.cell(), 0u32.wrappingSub(value), code(order)) };\n\n    fn cell(borrow this) -> *mut u32 => borrow this.value as *u32 as *mut u32;\n}\n\n/// A usize that threads may read and change at once, as AtomicU64 (usize is 64 bits on every target, §29.3).\n@threadSafe\n@noncopy\npublic struct AtomicUSize {\n    private inner: AtomicU64;\n\n    /// A new atomic holding `value`.\n    public fn create(value: usize) -> AtomicUSize => AtomicUSize { inner: AtomicU64.create(value as u64) };\n\n    /// The same as create, in a static's initialiser (type-system.md §39.4).\n    @constant\n    public fn constant(value: usize) -> AtomicUSize => AtomicUSize { inner: AtomicU64.constant(value as u64) };\n\n    /// The value, sequentially consistent.\n    public fn load(borrow this) -> usize => truncate<usize>(this.inner.load());\n\n    /// Stores `value`, sequentially consistent.\n    public fn store(borrow this, value: usize) { this.inner.store(value as u64); }\n\n    /// Stores `value` and returns the old value, sequentially consistent.\n    public fn swap(borrow this, value: usize) -> usize => truncate<usize>(this.inner.swap(value as u64));\n\n    /// Ok(the old value) if it was `expected` and is now `desired`; else Err(the value it has).\n    public fn compareExchange(borrow this, expected: usize, desired: usize) -> Result<usize, usize> {\n        match (this.inner.compareExchange(expected as u64, desired as u64)) {\n            Ok(old) => { return Ok(truncate<usize>(old)); }\n            Err(old) => { return Err(truncate<usize>(old)); }\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// Adds, wrapping; returns the old value.\n    public fn fetchAdd(borrow this, value: usize) -> usize => truncate<usize>(this.inner.fetchAdd(value as u64));\n\n    /// Subtracts, wrapping; returns the old value.\n    public fn fetchSub(borrow this, value: usize) -> usize => truncate<usize>(this.inner.fetchSub(value as u64));\n\n    /// As load, with `order`.\n    public fn loadWith(borrow this, order: LoadOrdering) -> usize => truncate<usize>(this.inner.loadWith(order));\n\n  ",
    5 =>
        "  /// As store, with `order`.\n    public fn storeWith(borrow this, value: usize, order: StoreOrdering) { this.inner.storeWith(value as u64, order); }\n\n    /// As swap, with `order`.\n    public fn swapWith(borrow this, value: usize, order: Ordering) -> usize =>\n        truncate<usize>(this.inner.swapWith(value as u64, order));\n\n    /// As compareExchange, with `order` (a failure orders as a load, §38.3).\n    public fn compareExchangeWith(\n        borrow this,\n        expected: usize,\n        desired: usize,\n        order: Ordering,\n    ) -> Result<usize, usize> {\n        match (this.inner.compareExchangeWith(expected as u64, desired as u64, order)) {\n            Ok(old) => { return Ok(truncate<usize>(old)); }\n            Err(old) => { return Err(truncate<usize>(old)); }\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// As fetchAdd, with `order`.\n    public fn fetchAddWith(borrow this, value: usize, order: Ordering) -> usize =>\n        truncate<usize>(this.inner.fetchAddWith(value as u64, order));\n\n    /// As fetchSub, with `order`.\n    public fn fetchSubWith(borrow this, value: usize, order: Ordering) -> usize =>\n        truncate<usize>(this.inner.fetchSubWith(value as u64, order));\n}\n\n/// A bool that threads may read and change at once.\n@threadSafe\n@noncopy\npublic struct AtomicBool {\n    private inner: AtomicU32;\n\n    /// A new atomic holding `value`.\n    public fn create(value: bool) -> AtomicBool => AtomicBool { inner: AtomicU32.create(if (value) 1 else 0) };\n\n    /// The same as create, in a static's initialiser (type-system.md §39.4).\n    @constant\n    public fn constant(value: bool) -> AtomicBool => AtomicBool { inner: AtomicU32.constant(value as u32) };\n\n    /// The value, sequentially consistent.\n    public fn load(borrow this) -> bool => this.inner.load() != 0;\n\n    /// Stores `value`, sequentially consistent.\n    public fn store(borrow this, value: bool) { this.inner.store(if (value) 1 else 0); }\n\n    /// Stores `value` and returns the old value, sequentially consistent.\n    public fn swap(borrow this, value: bool) -> bool => this.inner.swap(if (value) 1 else 0) != 0;\n\n    /// Ok(the old value) if it was `expected` and is now `desired`; else Err(the value it has).\n    public fn compareExchange(borrow this, expected: bool, desired: bool) -> Result<bool, bool> {\n        match (this.inner.compareExchange(if (expected) 1 else 0, if (desired) 1 else 0)) {\n            Ok(old) => { return Ok(old != 0); }\n            Err(old) => { return Err(old != 0); }\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// As load, with `order`.\n    public fn loadWith(borrow this, order: LoadOrdering) -> bool => this.inner.loadWith(order) != 0;\n\n    /// As store, with `order`.\n    public fn storeWith(borrow this, value: bool, order: StoreOrdering) {\n        this.inner.storeWith(if (value) 1 else 0, order);\n    }\n\n    /// As swap, with `order`.\n    public fn swapWith(borrow this, value: bool, order: Ordering) -> bool =>\n        this.inner.swapWith(if (value) 1 else 0, order) != 0;\n\n    /// As compareExchange, with `order` (a failure orders as a load, §38.3).\n    public fn compareExchangeWith(borrow this, expected: bool, desired: bool, order: Ordering) -> Result<bool, bool> {\n        match (this.inner.compareExchangeWith(if (expected) 1 else 0, if (desired) 1 else 0, order)) {\n            Ok(old) => { return Ok(old != 0); }\n            Err(old) => { return Err(old != 0); }\n        }\n    }\n}\n",
    6 =>
        "// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only WITH Classpath-exception-2.0\n// lib/core: memory-mapped I/O (type-system.md §29.2). Bundled with ceesafec and part of every package.\n\n/// A cell read and written only by volatile accesses: a hardware register, or memory shared with a device.\n/// Not Copy: a copy would read the register with an ordinary access, once, instead of each time it is used.\n@noncopy\npublic struct Volatile<T: Copy> {\n    value: T;\n\n    /// Safety: `this.value` is a live, aligned `T` (it is a field), and the read is a single volatile access.\n    public fn read(borrow this) -> T {\n        let p = borrow this.value as *T;\n        return unsafe { p.readVolatile() };\n    }\n\n    /// Safety: as `read`; `borrow mut this` makes the write exclusive.\n    public fn write(borrow mut this, v: T) {\n        let p = borrow mut this.value as *mut T;\n        unsafe { p.writeVolatile(v); }\n    }\n}\n\n/// The address of a memory-mapped `T`, typically a `@repr(C)` struct of `Volatile` registers. Not Copy: two\n/// copies would be two owners of the same registers.\n@noncopy\npublic struct MmioPtr<T> {\n    base: *mut T;\n\n    /// Safety: `address` is mapped, aligned for `T`, and no other `MmioPtr` or reference aliases the registers.\n    public unsafe fn at(address: usize) -> MmioPtr<T> => MmioPtr<T> { base: address as *mut T };\n\n    /// Safety: `at`'s contract makes `base` a live `T` reached only through this value; the result borrows `this`.\n    public fn get(borrow this) -> borrow T => unsafe { Ptr.toBorrow(this.base) };\n\n    /// Safety: as `get`; `borrow mut this` makes the access exclusive.\n    public fn getMut(borrow mut this) -> borrow mut T => unsafe { Ptr.toBorrowMut(this.base) };\n\n    /// The address of the registers, as given to `at`.\n    public fn address(borrow this) -> usize => this.base as usize;\n}\n",
    7 =>
        "// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only WITH Classpath-exception-2.0\n// lib/core: methods of slices and vectors that take closures (type-system.md §36.4). Bundled with ceesafec and part of\n// every package. `s.sortBy(cmp)` is `SliceAlgorithms<T>.sortBy(s, cmp)`, and so on; `v.retain(keep)` is\n// `SliceAlgorithms<T>.retain` on the vector's elements, then `v.truncate` to the count it returns.\n\n/// The two parts of a slice that `splitAt` divides (type-system.md §16.5): the elements before the split, and from it on.\npublic struct SliceHalves<region r, T> {\n    public left: Slice<r, T>;\n    public right: Slice<r, T>;\n}\n\n/// The two parts of a mutable slice that `splitAtMut` divides: disjoint, so both may be written at once.\npublic struct SliceHalvesMut<region r, T> {\n    public left: SliceMut<r, T>;\n    public right: SliceMut<r, T>;\n}\n\n/// Splitting a slice in two (type-system.md §16.5): `s.splitAt(mid)` is `SliceSplitting<T>.splitAt(s, mid)`. The\n/// struct's region ties the halves to the slice they come from; it has no fields and is never made.\npublic struct SliceSplitting<region r, T> {\n    /// The elements before `mid`, and from `mid` on; panics if `mid` is past the end.\n    public fn splitAt(s: Slice<r, T>, mid: usize) -> SliceHalves<r, T> =>\n        SliceHalves { left: s[..mid], right: s[mid..] };\n\n    /// As splitAt, for a mutable slice: two mutable slices that do not overlap.\n    /// Safety: slicing `s[mid..]` checks `mid` against the length (it panics past the end), so `[0, mid)` and\n    /// `[mid, length)` are inside `s` and disjoint, and each is lent for as long as `s` is.\n    public fn splitAtMut(s: SliceMut<r, T>, mid: usize) -> SliceHalvesMut<r, T> {\n        let length = s.length;\n        let tail = s[mid..].length;\n        let start = s.ptr as *mut T;\n        return SliceHalvesMut {\n            left: unsafe { SliceMut.fromRaw(start, length - tail) },\n            right: unsafe { SliceMut.fromRaw(start.add(length - tail), tail) },\n        };\n    }\n}\n\n/// The algorithms over a slice of `T`, as static functions: the struct has no fields and is never made.\npublic struct SliceAlgorithms<T> {\n    /// Orders `s` so that `cmp` of each element and the next is at most zero, for a consistent `cmp`. Not stable.\n    /// An introsort: quicksort with a median-of-three pivot, insertion sort for short runs, and heapsort past a\n    /// depth of 2·log₂ n, so O(n log n) calls of `cmp` in the worst case. Allocates nothing; the recursion is on\n    /// the shorter side, so the stack is O(log n). An inconsistent `cmp` leaves some permutation of `s`.\n    @effectsOf(cmp)\n    public fn sortBy(s: SliceMut<T>, cmp: borrow mut fn(borrow T, borrow T) -> i32) {\n        var depth: u32 = 0;\n        var n = s.length;\n        while (n > 1) {\n            depth += 2;\n            n = n >> 1;\n        }\n        let length = s.length;\n        SliceAlgorithms<T>.introsort(s, 0, length, depth, cmp);\n    }\n\n    /// Sorts `s[lo..hi]`, with `depth` partitions left before heapsort takes over.\n    @effectsOf(cmp)\n    fn introsort(s: SliceMut<T>, lo: usize, hi: usize, depth: u32, cmp: borrow mut fn(borrow T, borrow T) -> i32) {\n        var low = lo;\n        var high = hi;\n        var left = depth;\n        while (high - low > 16) {\n            if (left == 0) {\n                SliceAlgorithms<T>.heapsort(s, low, high, cmp);\n                return;\n            }\n            left -= 1;\n            let p = SliceAlgorithms<T>.partition(s, low, high, cmp);\n            // Recurse into the shorter side and loop on the longer one: at most log₂ n frames.\n            if (p - low < high - p) {\n                SliceAlgorithms<T>.introsort(s, low, p, left, cmp);\n                low = p + 1;\n            } else {\n                SliceAlgorithms<T>.introsort(s, p + 1, high, left, cmp);\n                high = p;\n            }\n        }\n        SliceAlgorithms<T>.insertion(s, low, high, cmp);\n    }\n\n    /// Partitions `s[lo..hi]` (at least 3 elements) around a median-of-three p",
    8 =>
        "ivot; returns the pivot's index.\n    /// Everything before it compares below the pivot, nothing after it does.\n    @effectsOf(cmp)\n    fn partition(s: SliceMut<T>, lo: usize, hi: usize, cmp: borrow mut fn(borrow T, borrow T) -> i32) -> usize {\n        let last = hi - 1;\n        let mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;\n        // Order s[lo], s[mid], s[last]; the median goes to `last`, the pivot's place while partitioning.\n        if (cmp(borrow s[mid], borrow s[lo]) < 0) { s.swap(mid, lo); }\n        if (cmp(borrow s[last], borrow s[mid]) < 0) {\n            s.swap(last, mid);\n            if (cmp(borrow s[mid], borrow s[lo]) < 0) { s.swap(mid, lo); }\n        }\n        s.swap(mid, last);\n        var store = lo;\n        for (i in lo..last) {\n            if (cmp(borrow s[i], borrow s[last]) < 0) {\n                s.swap(i, store);\n                store += 1;\n            }\n        }\n        s.swap(store, last);\n        return store;\n    }\n\n    /// Insertion sort of `s[lo..hi]`, by adjacent swaps.\n    @effectsOf(cmp)\n    fn insertion(s: SliceMut<T>, lo: usize, hi: usize, cmp: borrow mut fn(borrow T, borrow T) -> i32) {\n        for (i in lo + 1..hi) {\n            var j = i;\n            while (j > lo && cmp(borrow s[j], borrow s[j - 1]) < 0) {\n                s.swap(j, j - 1);\n                j -= 1;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// Heapsort of `s[lo..hi]`: a max-heap, then the largest moved to the end, one at a time.\n    @effectsOf(cmp)\n    fn heapsort(s: SliceMut<T>, lo: usize, hi: usize, cmp: borrow mut fn(borrow T, borrow T) -> i32) {\n        let n = hi - lo;\n        var start = n / 2;\n        while (start > 0) {\n            start -= 1;\n            SliceAlgorithms<T>.siftDown(s, lo, start, n, cmp);\n        }\n        var end = n;\n        while (end > 1) {\n            end -= 1;\n            s.swap(lo, lo + end);\n            SliceAlgorithms<T>.siftDown(s, lo, 0, end, cmp);\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// Restores the heap property below `root` in the heap `s[base..base + n]`.\n    @effectsOf(cmp)\n    fn siftDown(s: SliceMut<T>, base: usize, root: usize, n: usize, cmp: borrow mut fn(borrow T, borrow T) -> i32) {\n        var parent = root;\n        while (2 * parent + 1 < n) {\n            var child = 2 * parent + 1;\n            if (child + 1 < n && cmp(borrow s[base + child], borrow s[base + child + 1]) < 0) {\n                child += 1;\n            }\n            if (cmp(borrow s[base + parent], borrow s[base + child]) >= 0) {\n                return;\n            }\n            s.swap(base + parent, base + child);\n            parent = child;\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// Calls `visit` on each element, in index order.\n    @effectsOf(visit)\n    public fn forEach(s: Slice<T>, visit: borrow mut fn(borrow T)) {\n        for (i in 0..s.length) { visit(borrow s[i]); }\n    }\n\n    /// Does `test` return true for some element? Stops at the first that does.\n    @effectsOf(test)\n    public fn any(s: Slice<T>, test: borrow mut fn(borrow T) -> bool) -> bool {\n        for (i in 0..s.length) {\n            if (test(borrow s[i])) { return true; }\n        }\n        return false;\n    }\n\n    /// Does `test` return true for every element? Stops at the first that does not.\n    @effectsOf(test)\n    public fn all(s: Slice<T>, test: borrow mut fn(borrow T) -> bool) -> bool {\n        for (i in 0..s.length) {\n            if (!test(borrow s[i])) { return false; }\n        }\n        return true;\n    }\n\n    /// Moves the elements for which `keep` returns true to the front, in order, calling it once on each element in\n    /// index order; returns how many there are. `v.retain(keep)` then truncates the vector to that length.\n    @effectsOf(keep)\n    public fn retain(s: SliceMut<T>, keep: borrow mut fn(borrow T) -> bool) -> usize {\n        var kept: usize = 0;\n        for (i in 0..s.length) {\n            if (keep(borrow s[i])) {\n                s.swap(kept, i);\n                kept += 1;\n            }\n        }\n        return kept;\n    }\n}\n",
    9 =>
        "// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only WITH Classpath-exception-2.0\n// Host services (type-system.md §26.7-§26.10, ADR 0028): running a program with its output captured, the kind of a\n// path, directories, a fresh temporary directory, the working directory and the environment. Hosted only, over\n// runtime/hosted/ceesafe_rt_io.c, whose calls return a value or a negated errno. `Process.capture` and `File.kind` are\n// the checker's names for HostServices.capture and HostServices.kind.\n\nextern \"C\" {\n    @effects()\n    fn ceesafe_host_env(name: *u8, length: u64, value: *mut (*u8), valueLength: *mut u64) -> i64;\n    @effects(sleep)\n    fn ceesafe_host_path_kind(path: *u8, length: u64) -> i64;\n    @effects(sleep)\n    fn ceesafe_host_dir_create(path: *u8, length: u64) -> i64;\n    @effects(sleep)\n    fn ceesafe_host_dir_remove(path: *u8, length: u64) -> i64;\n    @effects(sleep, alloc)\n    fn ceesafe_host_dir_open(path: *u8, length: u64, error: *mut i32) -> *mut void;\n    @effects(sleep)\n    fn ceesafe_host_dir_next(dir: *mut void, name: *mut (*u8), length: *mut u64) -> i64;\n    @effects(sleep)\n    fn ceesafe_host_dir_close(dir: *mut void);\n    @effects(sleep)\n    fn ceesafe_host_temporary(buffer: *mut u8, capacity: u64) -> i64;\n    @effects(sleep)\n    fn ceesafe_host_current(buffer: *mut u8, capacity: u64) -> i64;\n    @effects(sleep, alloc)\n    fn ceesafe_host_spawn(\n        arguments: *void,\n        count: u64,\n        input: *u8,\n        inputLength: u64,\n        output: *u8,\n        outputLength: u64,\n        errors: *u8,\n        errorsLength: u64,\n    ) -> i64;\n    @effects()\n    fn ceesafe_io_kind(error: i32) -> u32;\n}\n\n/// What is at a path (`File.kind`, type-system.md §26.8).\npublic enum PathKind {\n    File,\n    Directory,\n    Other,\n}\n\n/// A finished program's exit status and what it wrote (`Process.capture`, type-system.md §26.7).\npublic struct ProcessOutput {\n    /// The exit status, or 128 plus the signal that ended it, as `Process.run` reports it.\n    public status: i32;\n    public stdout: Vector<u8>;\n    public stderr: Vector<u8>;\n}\n\n/// The environment the program started with (type-system.md §26.9).\npublic struct Environment {\n    /// The value of the variable `name` as static text, or null if it is not set or not valid UTF-8.\n    /// Safety: the runtime returns getenv's text, which stays valid and unchanged: CeeSafe has no way to change the\n    /// environment (C code that calls setenv breaks this, as it does in C).\n    public fn variable(name: StringView) -> StringView? {\n        var value = 0 as *u8;\n        var length: u64 = 0;\n        let found = unsafe { ceesafe_host_env(\n            name.ptr,\n            name.length as u64,\n            borrow mut value as *mut (*u8),\n            borrow mut length as *mut u64,\n        ) };\n        if (found != 1) {\n            return null;\n        }\n        let bytes = unsafe { Slice.fromRaw(value, truncate<usize>(length)) };\n        return StringView.fromUtf8(bytes);\n    }\n}\n\n/// Directories (type-system.md §26.10).\npublic struct Directory {\n    /// Makes one directory; `AlreadyExists` if something is there already.\n    /// Safety: the runtime reads the path's bytes during the call only.\n    public fn create(path: StringView) -> Result<void, IoError> {\n        let result = unsafe { ceesafe_host_dir_create(path.ptr, path.length as u64) };\n        return if (result < 0) Err(HostServices.hostError(result)) else Ok();\n    }\n\n    /// Removes an empty directory.\n    /// Safety: the runtime reads the path's bytes during the call only.\n    public fn remove(path: StringView) -> Result<void, IoError> {\n        let result = unsafe { ceesafe_host_dir_remove(path.ptr, path.length as u64) };\n        return if (result < 0) Err(HostServices.hostError(result)) else Ok();\n    }\n\n    /// The names in a directory, without `.` and `..`, sorted by byte value so programs stay deterministic. A name\n    /// that is not valid UTF-8 is `InvalidInput`: skipping it could make a program miss a file wit",
    10 =>
        "hout a word.\n    /// Safety: the listing is closed on every path out (`defer`), and each name is copied before the next call\n    /// reuses the runtime's buffer.\n    public fn list(path: StringView) -> Result<Vector<String>, IoError> {\n        var error: i32 = 0;\n        let listing = unsafe { ceesafe_host_dir_open(path.ptr, path.length as u64, borrow mut error as *mut i32) };\n        if (listing.isNull()) {\n            return Err(HostServices.hostError(0 - (error as i64)));\n        }\n        defer {\n            unsafe { ceesafe_host_dir_close(listing); }\n        }\n        var names = Vector<String>.create();\n        loop {\n            var name = 0 as *u8;\n            var length: u64 = 0;\n            let next = unsafe { ceesafe_host_dir_next(\n                listing,\n                borrow mut name as *mut (*u8),\n                borrow mut length as *mut u64,\n            ) };\n            if (next == 0) {\n                break;\n            }\n            if (next < 0) {\n                return Err(HostServices.hostError(next));\n            }\n            let bytes = unsafe { Slice.fromRaw(name, truncate<usize>(length)) };\n            let text = match (StringView.fromUtf8(bytes)) {\n                Some(t) => t,\n                null => { return Err(IoError.InvalidInput); }\n            };\n            let copy = HostServices.stringOrIoError(HostServices.copyText(text))?;\n            HostServices.pushed(names.push(copy))?;\n        }\n        // An insertion sort rather than sortBy: an instance of lib/core's generic sort made here would be emitted in every\n        // hosted program, used or not.\n        for (i in 1..names.length) {\n            var j = i;\n            while (j > 0 && HostServices.compareText(names[j - 1], names[j]) > 0) {\n                names.swap(j - 1, j);\n                j -= 1;\n            }\n        }\n        return Ok(names);\n    }\n\n    /// A new, empty directory that only this user can enter, under the platform's temporary directory. The caller\n    /// removes it.\n    /// Safety: the runtime writes at most the buffer's length.\n    public fn temporary() -> Result<String, IoError> {\n        var buffer = [0u8; 4096];\n        let length = unsafe { ceesafe_host_temporary(borrow mut buffer[0] as *mut u8, 4096) };\n        return HostServices.textOf(buffer, length);\n    }\n\n    /// The working directory.\n    /// Safety: the runtime writes at most the buffer's length.\n    public fn current() -> Result<String, IoError> {\n        var buffer = [0u8; 4096];\n        let length = unsafe { ceesafe_host_current(borrow mut buffer[0] as *mut u8, 4096) };\n        return HostServices.textOf(buffer, length);\n    }\n}\n\n/// A program's argument as the runtime's `cee_str` (a struct is laid out by C's rules, type-system §12; not @repr(C), which would put it in every program's ceesafe_exports.h).\nstruct HostServicesText {\n    ptr: *u8;\n    len: u64;\n}\n\n/// The functions behind `Process.capture` and `File.kind`.\npublic struct HostServices {\n    /// `Process.capture(program, arguments, input)` (type-system.md §26.7): runs `program`, found on `PATH`, with\n    /// `arguments`, `input` as its standard input (empty if null), and returns its exit status and what it wrote to\n    /// standard output and standard error. The streams go through files in a private temporary directory, removed\n    /// before this returns, so a program that writes much to both cannot block (ADR 0028).\n    public fn capture(\n        program: StringView,\n        arguments: borrow Vector<StringView>,\n        input: Slice<u8>?,\n    ) -> Result<ProcessOutput, IoError> {\n        let directory = Directory.temporary()?;\n        let result = HostServices.captureIn(directory.view(), program, arguments, input);\n        HostServices.clean(directory.view());\n        return result;\n    }\n\n    /// `File.kind(path)` (type-system.md §26.8): what is at `path`, following symbolic links, or null if nothing is.\n    /// Safety: the runtime reads the path's bytes during the call only.\n    public",
    11 =>
        " fn kind(path: StringView) -> Result<PathKind?, IoError> {\n        let kind = unsafe { ceesafe_host_path_kind(path.ptr, path.length as u64) };\n        return match (kind) {\n            0 => Ok(null),\n            1 => Ok(PathKind.File),\n            2 => Ok(PathKind.Directory),\n            _ => if (kind < 0) Err(HostServices.hostError(kind)) else Ok(PathKind.Other),\n        };\n    }\n\n    /// Safety: `texts` holds a view per argument and outlives the call; the runtime reads it and the paths during\n    /// the call only.\n    fn captureIn(\n        directory: StringView,\n        program: StringView,\n        arguments: borrow Vector<StringView>,\n        input: Slice<u8>?,\n    ) -> Result<ProcessOutput, IoError> {\n        let inPath = HostServices.stringOrIoError(HostServices.pathIn(directory, \"in\"))?;\n        let outPath = HostServices.stringOrIoError(HostServices.pathIn(directory, \"out\"))?;\n        let errPath = HostServices.stringOrIoError(HostServices.pathIn(directory, \"err\"))?;\n        var file = File.create(inPath.view())?;\n        match (input) {\n            Some(bytes) => { file.write(bytes)?; }\n            null => {}\n        }\n        var texts = Vector<HostServicesText>.create();\n        HostServices.pushed(texts.push(unsafe { HostServicesText { ptr: program.ptr, len: program.length as u64 } }))?;\n        for (argument in arguments) {\n            HostServices.pushed(\n                texts.push(unsafe { HostServicesText { ptr: argument.ptr, len: argument.length as u64 } }),\n            )?;\n        }\n        let status = unsafe { ceesafe_host_spawn(\n            texts.view().ptr as *void,\n            texts.length as u64,\n            inPath.view().ptr,\n            inPath.length as u64,\n            outPath.view().ptr,\n            outPath.length as u64,\n            errPath.view().ptr,\n            errPath.length as u64,\n        ) };\n        if (status < 0) {\n            return Err(HostServices.hostError(status));\n        }\n        return Ok(\n            ProcessOutput {\n                status: truncate<i32>(status),\n                stdout: HostServices.readAll(outPath.view())?,\n                stderr: HostServices.readAll(errPath.view())?,\n            },\n        );\n    }\n\n    /// Removes the three files and the directory, ignoring what is not there.\n    fn clean(directory: StringView) {\n        for (k in 0..3) {\n            let name: StringView = match (k) {\n                0 => \"in\",\n                1 => \"out\",\n                _ => \"err\",\n            };\n            match (HostServices.pathIn(directory, name)) {\n                Ok(path) => { let removed = File.remove(path.view()); }\n                Err(_) => {}\n            }\n        }\n        let removed = Directory.remove(directory);\n    }\n\n    // The helpers below are this struct's, not lib/std's top-level names: every hosted package shares those, so a\n    // top-level `pathIn` or `readAll` here would clash with a program's own (N0201; fixed after 0.4.0).\n\n    /// The text of a runtime call that wrote `length` bytes into `buffer`, or its error.\n    fn textOf(buffer: [u8; 4096], length: i64) -> Result<String, IoError> {\n        if (length < 0) {\n            return Err(HostServices.hostError(length));\n        }\n        let bytes = buffer;\n        let text = match (StringView.fromUtf8(bytes[..truncate<usize>(length)])) {\n            Some(t) => t,\n            null => { return Err(IoError.InvalidInput); }\n        };\n        return HostServices.stringOrIoError(HostServices.copyText(text));\n    }\n\n    /// `directory/name`.\n    fn pathIn(directory: StringView, name: StringView) -> Result<String, AllocError> {\n        var path = String.create();\n        path.append(directory)?;\n        path.append(\"/\")?;\n        path.append(name)?;\n        return Ok(path);\n    }\n\n    fn copyText(text: StringView) -> Result<String, AllocError> {\n        var copy = String.create();\n        copy.append(text)?;\n        return Ok(copy);\n    }\n\n    fn stringOrIoError(result: Result<String, AllocError>) -> Result",
    12 =>
        "<String, IoError> => match (result) {\n        Ok(value) => Ok(value),\n        Err(_) => Err(IoError.OutOfMemory),\n    };\n\n    /// As stringOrIoError, for `void` results (a generic copy with T = void does not check, type-system.md §20.4).\n    fn pushed(result: Result<void, AllocError>) -> Result<void, IoError> => match (result) {\n        Ok() => Ok(),\n        Err(_) => Err(IoError.OutOfMemory),\n    };\n\n    /// The whole file's bytes.\n    fn readAll(path: StringView) -> Result<Vector<u8>, IoError> {\n        var file = File.open(path)?;\n        var bytes = Vector<u8>.create();\n        var buffer = [0u8; 65536];\n        loop {\n            let n = file.read(mut buffer[..])?;\n            if (n == 0) {\n                break;\n            }\n            for (i in 0..n) {\n                match (bytes.push(buffer[i])) {\n                    Ok() => {}\n                    Err(_) => { return Err(IoError.OutOfMemory); }\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        return Ok(bytes);\n    }\n\n    /// Byte-wise order of two strings: negative, zero or positive.\n    fn compareText(a: borrow String, b: borrow String) -> i32 {\n        let n = if (a.length < b.length) a.length else b.length;\n        for (i in 0..n) {\n            if (a[i] != b[i]) {\n                return if (a[i] < b[i]) -1 else 1;\n            }\n        }\n        return if (a.length < b.length) -1 else if (a.length > b.length) 1 else 0;\n    }\n\n    /// The IoError for a negated errno from the runtime (its kinds are CEE_IO_* in rt.h, in IoError's variant order).\n    /// Safety: ceesafe_io_kind only classifies the number.\n    fn hostError(code: i64) -> IoError {\n        let error = truncate<i32>(0 - code);\n        return match (unsafe { ceesafe_io_kind(error) }) {\n            0 => IoError.NotFound,\n            1 => IoError.PermissionDenied,\n            2 => IoError.AlreadyExists,\n            3 => IoError.IsDirectory,\n            4 => IoError.NotDirectory,\n            5 => IoError.InvalidInput,\n            6 => IoError.BrokenPipe,\n            7 => IoError.ConnectionRefused,\n            8 => IoError.AddressInUse,\n            9 => IoError.OutOfMemory,\n            _ => IoError.Other(error),\n        };\n    }\n}\n",
    13 =>
        "// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only WITH Classpath-exception-2.0\n// Shared state between threads (type-system.md §38.3, ADR 0016): Arc and Mutex, over the hosted runtime\n// (runtime/hosted/ceesafe_rt_thread.c). Their unsafe code keeps ownership.md T1–T3. The atomics are in lib/core (ADR 0026).\n\nextern \"C\" {\n    @effects(alloc)\n    fn ceesafe_mutex_create() -> *mut void;\n    @effects(sleep)\n    fn ceesafe_mutex_lock(m: *mut void);\n    @effects()\n    fn ceesafe_mutex_unlock(m: *mut void);\n    @effects()\n    fn ceesafe_mutex_destroy(m: *mut void);\n}\n\n/// Mutual exclusion: the data is reached only through a guard, which one thread holds at a time.\n@threadSafe\n@noncopy\npublic struct Mutex<T: Send> {\n    private raw: *mut void;\n    private data: T;\n\n    /// A mutex holding `data`; its platform lock is allocated.\n    /// Safety: a null lock is reported, never stored.\n    public fn create(data: T) -> Result<Mutex<T>, AllocError> {\n        let raw = unsafe { ceesafe_mutex_create() };\n        if (raw.isNull()) {\n            return Err(AllocError.OutOfMemory);\n        }\n        return Ok(Mutex { raw: raw, data: data });\n    }\n\n    /// Waits for the lock; the guard lends the data until it is dropped.\n    /// Safety: `raw` is the lock made in create, destroyed only in drop, when no guard can be live (B-rules).\n    public fn lock(borrow this) -> MutexGuard<T> {\n        unsafe { ceesafe_mutex_lock(this.raw); }\n        return MutexGuard { lock: this };\n    }\n\n    /// Safety: no guard is live once the mutex can be dropped, so the lock is free.\n    drop {\n        unsafe { ceesafe_mutex_destroy(this.raw); }\n    }\n}\n\n/// Holding a Mutex: `data()` lends the protected value; dropping the guard unlocks.\npublic struct MutexGuard<region r, T: Send> {\n    private lock: borrow(r) Mutex<T>;\n\n    /// Safety: the guard holds the lock, so no other thread reaches the data, and the loan ends before the guard's drop.\n    public fn data(borrow mut this) -> borrow mut T =>\n        unsafe { Ptr.toBorrowMut(borrow this.lock.data as *T as *mut T) };\n\n    /// Safety: the guard took the lock in Mutex.lock and releases it once.\n    drop {\n        unsafe { ceesafe_mutex_unlock(this.lock.raw); }\n    }\n}\n\nstruct ArcInner<T> {\n    count: AtomicU64;\n    value: T;\n}\n\n/// Shared ownership across threads: each clone is an owner, and the last owner's drop destroys the value.\n@threadSafe\n@noncopy\npublic struct Arc<T: Share> {\n    private inner: *mut ArcInner<T>;\n\n    /// Safety: the pointer comes from Box.intoRaw and is given back once, by the last owner's drop.\n    public fn create(value: T) -> Result<Arc<T>, AllocError> {\n        let boxed = Box.create(ArcInner { count: AtomicU64.create(1), value: value })?;\n        return Ok(Arc { inner: Box.intoRaw(boxed) });\n    }\n\n    /// Another owner of the same value.\n    /// Safety: this owner keeps the value alive while the count is raised.\n    public fn clone(borrow this) -> Arc<T> {\n        this.shared().count.fetchAdd(1);\n        return Arc { inner: this.inner };\n    }\n\n    /// The shared value, for as long as this owner is borrowed.\n    /// Safety: the value lives while any owner does; T is Share, so shared references to it may cross threads.\n    public fn get(borrow this) -> borrow T => borrow this.shared().value;\n\n    /// Safety: as get.\n    fn shared(borrow this) -> borrow ArcInner<T> => unsafe { Ptr.toBorrow(this.inner as *ArcInner<T>) };\n\n    /// Safety: only the owner that brings the count from one to zero gives the box back.\n    drop {\n        if (this.shared().count.fetchSub(1) == 1) {\n            let last: Box<ArcInner<T>> = unsafe { Box.fromRaw(this.inner) };\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n/// Why Thread.spawn failed: the thread, its record or its closure could not be allocated (type-system.md §38.2).\npublic enum SpawnError {\n    Resources,\n}\n",
    14 =>
        "// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only WITH Classpath-exception-2.0\n// Typed object caches over CosmoOS's slab caches (ADR 0027; kernel/include/kernel/kmalloc.h:47-53). A cache is a\n// static, set up once at start-up, as CosmoOS makes its own (kernel-services/network/mbuf.c); it is never destroyed,\n// so its boxes cannot outlive it.\n\n@header(\"kernel/kmalloc.h\")\nextern \"C\" {\n    struct kmem_cache;\n    // kmem_cache_create allocates its first slab's bookkeeping; like kmalloc it does not sleep (kmalloc.h).\n    @effects(alloc)\n    fn kmem_cache_create(name: *CChar, objectSize: usize, align: usize) -> *mut kmem_cache;\n    @effects()\n    fn kmem_cache_destroy(cache: *mut kmem_cache);\n    @effects(alloc)\n    fn kmem_cache_alloc(cache: *mut kmem_cache, flags: u32) -> *mut void;\n    @effects()\n    fn kmem_cache_free(cache: *mut kmem_cache, object: *mut void);\n}\n\n/// A slab cache of `T`s: `static CONNS: ObjectCache<Conn> = ObjectCache<Conn>.constant();`, then\n/// `CONNS.setUp(\"conn\\0\".ptr as *CChar)` once at start-up, then `CONNS.alloc(value)`. In v1 `T` holds no C state: a\n/// cache box moves its value out to drop it, and C state never moves (type-system.md §27.3).\n@threadSafe\n@noncopy\npublic struct ObjectCache<T: Send> {\n    /// The kmem_cache's address, 0 until setUp; written once, then only read.\n    private cache: AtomicUSize;\n\n    /// An empty cache for a static (type-system.md §39).\n    @constant\n    public fn constant() -> ObjectCache<T> => ObjectCache { cache: AtomicUSize.constant(0) };\n\n    /// Makes the slab cache, sized and aligned for `T`. `name` is a static string ending in a zero byte, which the cache\n    /// keeps. A second call keeps the first cache and returns Ok.\n    /// Safety: kmem_cache_create copies nothing and keeps `name`, which the caller keeps alive (a static string), and\n    /// its alignment (KMALLOC_MIN_ALIGN for 0) meets `T`'s;\n    /// a cache made by a racing call that lost is destroyed at once, before any object was allocated from it.\n    public fn setUp(borrow this, name: *CChar) -> Result<void, AllocError> {\n        if (this.cache.loadWith(LoadOrdering.Acquire) != 0) {\n            return Ok();\n        }\n        let layout = layoutOf<T>();\n        // CosmoOS refuses an alignment below KMALLOC_MIN_ALIGN (16), and 0 asks for it (slab.c cache_setup): its\n        // objects are then 16-byte aligned, which meets any smaller alignment.\n        let align = if (layout.align <= 16) 0 else layout.align;\n        let made = unsafe { kmem_cache_create(name, layout.size, align) };\n        if (made.isNull()) {\n            return Err(AllocError.OutOfMemory);\n        }\n        match (this.cache.compareExchangeWith(0, made as usize, Ordering.AcquireRelease)) {\n            Ok(previous) => {}\n            Err(other) => {\n                unsafe { kmem_cache_destroy(made); }\n            }\n        }\n        return Ok();\n    }\n\n    /// `value` in an object from this cache, or Err (and `value` dropped) if setUp has not run or the cache is out of\n    /// memory.\n    /// Safety: kmem_cache_alloc returns NULL or an unused object of the cache's size and alignment, which are `T`'s, so\n    /// the write is to valid, unshared storage; the box owns it from then on.\n    public fn alloc(borrow this, value: T) -> Result<CacheBox<T>, AllocError> {\n        let cache = this.cache.loadWith(LoadOrdering.Acquire) as *mut kmem_cache;\n        if (cache.isNull()) {\n            return Err(AllocError.OutOfMemory);\n        }\n        let object = unsafe { kmem_cache_alloc(cache, 0) } as *mut T;\n        if (object.isNull()) {\n            return Err(AllocError.OutOfMemory);\n        }\n        unsafe { object.write(value); }\n        return Ok(CacheBox { object: object, cache: cache });\n    }\n}\n\n/// A `T` in an object from an ObjectCache: the box owns it, and dropping the box drops the value and returns the\n/// object to its cache. Two pointers; not `Send` in v1, like a KernelHeap box (type-system.md §38.1).\n@noncopy\npublic struct CacheBox<T: Send> {\n ",
    15 =>
        "   private object: *mut T;\n    private cache: *mut kmem_cache;\n\n    /// The value.\n    /// Safety: `object` holds the box's `T` until the box's drop, and the reference is tied to the box (§13.5).\n    public fn get(borrow this) -> borrow T => unsafe { Ptr.toBorrow(this.object as *T) };\n\n    /// The value, for changing it.\n    /// Safety: as get; the box is borrowed mutably, so this is the only reference.\n    public fn getMut(borrow mut this) -> borrow mut T => unsafe { Ptr.toBorrowMut(this.object) };\n\n    /// Safety: `object` holds a `T` written by alloc and read out only here, once; the object goes back to the cache\n    /// it came from, which is never destroyed (setUp's caches live as long as the kernel).\n    drop {\n        let value = unsafe { this.object.read() };\n        unsafe { kmem_cache_free(this.cache, this.object as *mut void); }\n    }\n}\n",
    16 =>
        "// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only WITH Classpath-exception-2.0\n// The kernel heap (ADR 0025; from C5, docs/cosmoos/c5-report.md, type-system.md §30). CosmoOS's allocator is used as\n// it is: kmalloc and kfree, both exported (kernel/memory/kmalloc.c).\n\n@header(\"kernel/kmalloc.h\")\nextern \"C\" {\n    // Effects (type-system.md §32): kmalloc never sleeps (kmalloc.h: \"non-blocking and interrupt-safe\"), so it is\n    // allowed in atomic contexts; whether an IRQ handler may allocate is KQ-1, and CeeSafe's @context(irq) says no.\n    @effects(alloc)\n    fn kmalloc(size: usize, flags: u32) -> *mut void;\n    @effects()\n    fn kfree(p: *mut void);\n}\n\n/// CosmoOS's kernel heap (kernel/include/kernel/kmalloc.h): non-blocking and interrupt-safe; blocks are at least\n/// 16-byte aligned; at most KMALLOC_MAX_SIZE (4 MiB). `Box<T, KernelHeap>` is one pointer, freed with kfree.\npublic struct KernelHeap {}\n\nimpl Allocator for KernelHeap {\n    /// Safety: kmalloc returns null or a block of `layout.size` bytes, 16-byte aligned (kmalloc.h); larger\n    /// alignments are refused here, so an Ok block always meets `layout`.\n    fn allocate(borrow this, layout: Layout) -> Result<*mut u8, AllocError> {\n        if (layout.align > 16) {\n            return Err(AllocError.OutOfMemory);\n        }\n        let p = unsafe { kmalloc(layout.size, 0) } as *mut u8;\n        if (p.isNull()) {\n            return Err(AllocError.OutOfMemory);\n        }\n        return Ok(p);\n    }\n\n    /// Safety: the caller (Box) passes a block from `allocate`, once: kfree's contract (kmalloc.h).\n    unsafe fn deallocate(borrow this, p: *mut u8, layout: Layout) {\n        kfree(p as *mut void);\n    }\n}\n",
    17 =>
        "// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only WITH Classpath-exception-2.0\n// Kernel objects (ADR 0025; from C7d). A CeeSafe-defined kernel object embeds CosmoOS's own struct kobject (kernel/include/kernel/object.h)\n// and its struct kobject_type; KRef<T> is an owned, counted reference to one (kobject_get / kobject_put). The last\n// put runs the type's release, a generated C callback (type-system.md §34.1) that drops the CeeSafe data and frees the\n// object. CeeSafe never frees a kernel object any other way.\n//\n// CosmoOS's rules these follow (kernel/object/object.c; audit in docs/cosmoos/c7-report.md):\n// - kobject_init(obj, type) needs a type with a release; the count starts at 1; the kernel keeps `type`, so it lives\n//   inside the object here, and nothing reads it after release returns (kobject_release_final, object.c:61-72);\n// - the object's owner is the module whose text holds `release` (module_owner_of): module unload waits for it;\n// - the final put runs release, which may block, and nothing checks that it is not done under a spinlock: here\n//   kobject_put has the `sleep` effect, so a final put under a spin guard does not compile (type-system.md §32).\n\n@header(\"kernel/object.h\")\nextern \"C\" {\n    struct kobject;\n    struct kobject_type {\n        name: *CChar;\n        release: extern \"C\" fn(*mut kobject);\n        flags: u32;\n    }\n    @effects()\n    fn kobject_init(obj: *mut kobject, kind: *kobject_type);\n    @effects()\n    fn kobject_get(obj: *mut kobject);\n    @effects(sleep)\n    fn kobject_put(obj: *mut kobject);\n    @effects()\n    fn kobject_refcount(obj: *kobject) -> u32;\n}\n\n/// A kernel object: CosmoOS's kobject first (so the kernel's `struct kobject *` is the object's address, type-system.md\n/// §34.3), then its type, then the CeeSafe data. Made only by KRef<T>.create, and never moved.\npublic struct KObject<T> {\n    private obj: kobject;\n    private kind: kobject_type;\n    private name: *CChar;\n    private data: T;\n\n    /// Safety: runs once, at the object's final address, before anything else can see it (type-system.md §33.3); the\n    /// type it registers lives in the object, so it outlives every use the kernel makes of it.\n    @placed\n    fn init(borrow mut this) {\n        this.kind.name = this.name;\n        this.kind.release = release<T>;\n        unsafe { kobject_init(borrow mut this.obj as *mut kobject, borrow this.kind as *kobject_type); }\n    }\n}\n\n/// An owned reference to a kernel object. Cloning takes a count; dropping gives one back, and the last one releases\n/// the object. The data is shared, as the object is: state that changes goes behind a lock inside it.\npublic struct KRef<T> {\n    private object: *mut KObject<T>;\n\n    /// Makes an object with one reference. `name` is its type's name: a static string ending in a zero byte.\n    public fn create(name: *CChar, data: T) -> Result<KRef<T>, AllocError> {\n        match (Box.createIn(KernelHeap {}, KObject { name: name, data: data })) {\n            Ok(object) => { return Ok(KRef { object: Box.intoRaw(object) }); }\n            Err(e) => { return Err(e); }\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// Another reference to the same object.\n    /// Safety: this reference holds a count, so the object is live, as kobject_get needs.\n    public fn clone(borrow this) -> KRef<T> {\n        unsafe { kobject_get(this.header()); }\n        return KRef { object: this.object };\n    }\n\n    /// The data, while this reference is borrowed.\n    /// Safety: the object lives as long as a reference to it does, and the data is only read through it.\n    public fn get(borrow this) -> borrow T {\n        let object = unsafe { Ptr.toBorrow(this.object as *KObject<T>) };\n        return borrow object.data;\n    }\n\n    /// The object's reference count (kobject_refcount), for tests and diagnostics.\n    /// Safety: as get.\n    public fn count(borrow this) -> u32 => unsafe { kobject_refcount(this.header() as *kobject) };\n\n    fn header(borrow this) -> *mut kobject => this.object as *mut kobject;\n\n    /// Safety: ",
    18 =>
        "this reference held one count, which goes back here; the last put runs release<T>.\n    drop {\n        unsafe { kobject_put(this.header()); }\n    }\n}\n\n/// The release of every KObject<T>: the last reference is gone, so the data is dropped and the object freed.\n/// Safety: the kernel calls this once per object, after its count reached zero (kobject_release_final), with the\n/// kobject that KRef<T>.create made as the first field of a Box<KObject<T>, KernelHeap>.\n@callback\nunsafe fn release<T>(obj: *mut kobject) {\n    let object: Box<KObject<T>, KernelHeap> = Box.fromRaw(obj as *mut KObject<T>);\n}\n",
    19 =>
        "// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only WITH Classpath-exception-2.0\n// Intrusive lists (ADR 0030): CosmoOS's struct list_node (kernel/include/kernel/list.h), a circular doubly linked\n// list whose head and nodes are embedded in other structs. Its helpers are list.h's static inline functions, which an\n// extern declaration may call (type-system.md §28.2). A node's struct is recovered with containerOf over offsetOf.\n//\n// A walk that may remove the node it is on reads the next node first, as list_for_each_entry_safe does:\n//\n//     var node = List.first(head);\n//     while (!node.isNull()) {\n//         let after = List.next(head, node);\n//         let entry = containerOf(node, offsetOf<wait_entry>(link)) as *mut wait_entry;\n//         ...                                  // may List.remove(node)\n//         node = after;\n//     }\n\n@header(\"kernel/list.h\")\nextern \"C\" {\n    struct list_node {\n        next: *mut list_node;\n        prev: *mut list_node;\n    }\n    @effects()\n    fn list_init(head: *mut list_node);\n    @effects()\n    fn list_empty(head: *list_node) -> bool;\n    @effects()\n    fn list_push_back(head: *mut list_node, node: *mut list_node);\n    @effects()\n    fn list_push_front(head: *mut list_node, node: *mut list_node);\n    @effects()\n    fn list_remove(node: *mut list_node);\n}\n\n/// The operations on an intrusive list. Every one is unsafe: the nodes are raw C state that other code links and\n/// unlinks, so the caller holds whatever lock protects the list and keeps every node alive while it is linked.\npublic struct List {\n    /// Makes `head` an empty list (it points to itself).\n    /// Safety: `head` is writable and on no list.\n    @effects()\n    public unsafe fn init(head: *mut list_node) => list_init(head);\n\n    /// Safety: `head` is an initialised list.\n    @effects()\n    public unsafe fn isEmpty(head: *list_node) -> bool => list_empty(head);\n\n    /// Safety: `head` is an initialised list and `node` is on none.\n    @effects()\n    public unsafe fn pushBack(head: *mut list_node, node: *mut list_node) => list_push_back(head, node);\n\n    /// Safety: `head` is an initialised list and `node` is on none.\n    @effects()\n    public unsafe fn pushFront(head: *mut list_node, node: *mut list_node) => list_push_front(head, node);\n\n    /// Unlinks `node` from its list (list.h leaves it pointing to itself).\n    /// Safety: `node` is on a list.\n    @effects()\n    public unsafe fn remove(node: *mut list_node) => list_remove(node);\n\n    /// The first node after `head`, or null when the list is empty.\n    /// Safety: `head` is an initialised list.\n    @effects()\n    public unsafe fn first(head: *list_node) -> *mut list_node {\n        let n = Ptr.toBorrow(head).next;\n        return if (n as *list_node == head) 0 as *mut list_node else n;\n    }\n\n    /// The node after `node` on the list `head`, or null when `node` is the last.\n    /// Safety: `head` is an initialised list and `node` is on it.\n    @effects()\n    public unsafe fn next(head: *list_node, node: *mut list_node) -> *mut list_node {\n        let n = Ptr.toBorrow(node as *list_node).next;\n        return if (n as *list_node == head) 0 as *mut list_node else n;\n    }\n}\n\n/// The address of the struct whose field at `offset` (offsetOf<T>(field)) is `node`: C's container_of, cast by the\n/// caller to `*mut T`. Not generic, since `T` is usually C-laid-out (it embeds the node), which no type argument may be\n/// (type-system.md §33.1).\n/// Safety: `node` is that field of a live struct.\n@effects()\npublic unsafe fn containerOf(node: *mut list_node, offset: usize) -> *mut void =>\n    (node as *mut u8).sub(offset) as *mut void;\n",
    20 =>
        "// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only WITH Classpath-exception-2.0\n// Quiescence (ADR 0025; from C7d; kernel/include/kernel/quiesce.h).\n// A read side is any preemption-disabled region, so holding a\n// spin guard (sync.cee) is one; a grace period ends when every CPU has passed a quiescent state. Readers that\n// found an object under a read section may use it until they leave the section, so an object unlinked from what\n// readers walk is freed only after a grace period: synchronously (Quiesce.synchronize, which sleeps) or deferred\n// (deferDrop, through call_quiesce and an embedded quiesce_head).\n//\n// Not here: explicit read sections (quiesce_read_lock/unlock) in modules. They are inline and call preempt_enable,\n// which CosmoOS does not export (KQ-2); a spin guard is a read section a module can take.\n\n@header(\"kernel/quiesce.h\")\nextern \"C\" {\n    struct quiesce_head;\n    @effects(sleep)\n    fn synchronize_quiesce();\n    @effects()\n    fn call_quiesce(head: *mut quiesce_head, f: extern \"C\" fn(*mut quiesce_head));\n}\n\npublic struct Quiesce {\n    /// Waits for a grace period over the CPUs online now. Sleeps, so it is not allowed under a spin guard.\n    /// Safety: synchronize_quiesce asks for nothing but a context that may sleep, which its effect enforces.\n    public fn synchronize() {\n        unsafe { synchronize_quiesce(); }\n    }\n}\n\n/// A value that can be freed after a grace period: its quiesce_head first (type-system.md §34.3), then the value.\n/// Made in a KernelHeap box and never moved.\npublic struct Deferred<T> {\n    private head: quiesce_head;\n    private data: T;\n\n    /// The head is zero-filled, as call_quiesce requires of a head that never ran.\n    public fn create(data: T) -> Result<Box<Deferred<T>, KernelHeap>, AllocError> =>\n        Box.createIn(KernelHeap {}, Deferred { data: data });\n\n    public fn get(borrow this) -> borrow T => borrow this.data;\n\n    public fn getMut(borrow mut this) -> borrow mut T => borrow mut this.data;\n}\n\n/// The deferred free of a Deferred<T>: drops the value and frees it, in thread context, after the grace period.\n/// Safety: call_quiesce passes the head that deferDrop gave it, the first field of a Box<Deferred<T>, KernelHeap>\n/// that deferDrop gave up, once.\n@callback\nunsafe fn reclaim<T>(head: *mut quiesce_head) {\n    let value: Box<Deferred<T>, KernelHeap> = Box.fromRaw(head as *mut Deferred<T>);\n}\n\n/// Drops `value` after a grace period that begins now; returns at once, in any context. The box is moved in, so\n/// nothing can use it, or submit it again, while the free is pending.\n/// Safety: no new reader can find the value any more (it was unlinked from everything readers walk), and the code of\n/// reclaim<T> stays loaded until the callback has run: module unload does not wait for pending callbacks\n/// (kernel/module/module.c:627-631; KQ-3).\npublic unsafe fn deferDrop<T>(value: Box<Deferred<T>, KernelHeap>) {\n    call_quiesce(Box.intoRaw(value) as *mut quiesce_head, reclaim<T>);\n}\n",
    21 =>
        "// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only WITH Classpath-exception-2.0\n// Locks, guards and per-CPU data (ADR 0025; the locks from C7c). CosmoOS's spinlocks and mutexes as CeeSafe types that hold the data they protect (docs/cosmoos/c7-report.md;\n// ADR 0009; type-system.md §32-§33). The lock is CosmoOS's own C state, imported from its headers and embedded by\n// value, so the C compiler lays it out and it never moves; the data is reachable only through a guard; holding a\n// spin guard is an atomic context, so CeeSafe code that could sleep there does not compile.\n//\n// CosmoOS's rules these follow (kernel/include/kernel/spinlock.h, mutex.h; audit in c7-report.md):\n// - spinlock_init(lock, name) before first use; `name` is the lockdep class name and is kept, so it is static;\n// - spin_lock disables preemption until spin_unlock; re-acquiring a held lock on the same CPU panics;\n// - spin_lock_irqsave also disables interrupts and returns the state that spin_unlock_irqrestore puts back;\n//   nested irqsave locks are released in the reverse order (guards in locals are dropped in that order);\n// - mutex_lock sleeps (it calls might_sleep), so it is forbidden with a spinlock held or in an interrupt;\n// - an initialised struct mutex points into itself (its wait queue's list heads), so it must never move.\n\n@header(\"kernel/spinlock.h\", \"kernel/mutex.h\", \"kernel/percpu.h\", \"arch/irq.h\", \"arch/cpu.h\")\nextern \"C\" {\n    @ctype(\"spinlock_t\")\n    struct spinlock_t;\n    struct mutex;\n    // Static initialisers (type-system.md §39.4): C macros that expand to a lock as spinlock_init and mutex_init\n    // would leave it. A mutex points into itself (its wait queue's and pi_link's list heads), so MUTEX_INIT_AT takes its\n    // own address, `here` (ADR 0027); CosmoOS's mutex.h defines it.\n    @constant\n    fn SPINLOCK_INIT(name: *CChar) -> spinlock_t;\n    @constant\n    fn MUTEX_INIT_AT(at: *mut mutex, name: *CChar) -> mutex;\n    @effects()\n    fn preempt_disable();\n    @effects()\n    fn preempt_enable();\n    // arch_irq_state_t is `unsigned long`, which is `size_t` on every CosmoOS target.\n    @effects()\n    fn arch_irq_save() -> usize;\n    @effects()\n    fn arch_irq_restore(state: usize);\n    @effects()\n    fn arch_cpu_id() -> u32;\n    @effects()\n    fn spinlock_init(lock: *mut spinlock_t, name: *CChar);\n    @effects()\n    fn spin_lock(lock: *mut spinlock_t);\n    @effects()\n    fn spin_unlock(lock: *mut spinlock_t);\n    // arch_irq_state_t is `unsigned long`, which is `size_t` on every CosmoOS target (and on the hosts).\n    @effects()\n    fn spin_lock_irqsave(lock: *mut spinlock_t) -> usize;\n    @effects()\n    fn spin_unlock_irqrestore(lock: *mut spinlock_t, state: usize);\n    @effects()\n    fn mutex_init(m: *mut mutex, name: *CChar);\n    @effects(sleep)\n    fn mutex_lock(m: *mut mutex);\n    @effects()\n    fn mutex_unlock(m: *mut mutex);\n}\n\n/// A spinlock and the data it protects. Make one with `SpinLock<T>.create(name, data)`: it holds C state, so it lives\n/// in its box and never moves (type-system.md §33). Its fields are private (type-system.md §12), so the data is\n/// reached only through a guard, and a guard exists only while the lock is held.\n@threadSafe\n@noncopy\npublic struct SpinLock<T: Send> {\n    private raw: spinlock_t;\n    /// The lockdep class name: a static string ending in a zero byte (spinlock_init keeps the pointer).\n    private name: *CChar;\n    private data: T;\n\n    /// A lock for a static (type-system.md §39): `static TABLE: SpinLock<Table> = SpinLock.constant(\"table\\0\".ptr as\n    /// *CChar, Table.empty());`. SPINLOCK_INIT leaves it as `init` would.\n    @constant\n    public fn constant(name: *CChar, data: T) -> SpinLock<T> =>\n        SpinLock { raw: SPINLOCK_INIT(name), name: name, data: data };\n\n    /// Makes one in a KernelHeap box, initialised in place before this returns (type-system.md §33.3). `name` is its\n    /// lockdep class name: a static string ending in a zero byte.\n    public fn create(name: *CChar, data: T) -> R",
    22 =>
        "esult<Box<SpinLock<T>, KernelHeap>, AllocError> =>\n        Box.createIn(KernelHeap {}, SpinLock { name: name, data: data });\n\n    /// Safety: runs once, at the lock's final address, before anything else can see it (type-system.md §33.3).\n    @placed\n    fn init(borrow mut this) {\n        unsafe { spinlock_init(borrow mut this.raw as *mut spinlock_t, this.name); }\n    }\n\n    /// Takes the lock, spinning; preemption is off until the guard is dropped.\n    /// Safety: `raw` was initialised by `init`, and spin_lock needs nothing else.\n    public fn lock(borrow this) -> SpinGuard<T> {\n        unsafe { spin_lock(this.cLock()); }\n        return SpinGuard { lock: this };\n    }\n\n    /// The C lock. Locking mutates it through a shared reference: the C code synchronises that itself.\n    fn cLock(borrow this) -> *mut spinlock_t => borrow this.raw as *spinlock_t as *mut spinlock_t;\n}\n\n/// Holding the lock. An atomic context while it is live (type-system.md §32.3); unlocks when dropped.\n@context(atomic)\npublic struct SpinGuard<region r, T: Send> {\n    private lock: borrow(r) SpinLock<T>;\n\n    /// The protected data, for as long as the guard is borrowed.\n    /// Safety: the lock is held, so this guard is the only way to the data until it is dropped: every access goes\n    /// through a guard, and a second guard cannot exist meanwhile (spin_lock does not return, and panics on the\n    /// same CPU).\n    public fn data(borrow mut this) -> borrow mut T =>\n        unsafe { Ptr.toBorrowMut(borrow this.lock.data as *T as *mut T) };\n\n    /// Safety: the guard holds the lock, which it took in `lock`.\n    drop {\n        unsafe { spin_unlock(this.lock.cLock()); }\n    }\n}\n\n/// A spinlock that also disables interrupts while held, for data an interrupt handler shares.\n@threadSafe\n@noncopy\npublic struct IrqSpinLock<T: Send> {\n    private raw: spinlock_t;\n    private name: *CChar;\n    private data: T;\n\n    /// A lock for a static, as SpinLock.constant.\n    @constant\n    public fn constant(name: *CChar, data: T) -> IrqSpinLock<T> =>\n        IrqSpinLock { raw: SPINLOCK_INIT(name), name: name, data: data };\n\n    /// Makes one in a KernelHeap box, initialised in place before this returns (type-system.md §33.3). `name` is its\n    /// lockdep class name: a static string ending in a zero byte.\n    public fn create(name: *CChar, data: T) -> Result<Box<IrqSpinLock<T>, KernelHeap>, AllocError> =>\n        Box.createIn(KernelHeap {}, IrqSpinLock { name: name, data: data });\n\n    /// Safety: as SpinLock.init.\n    @placed\n    fn init(borrow mut this) {\n        unsafe { spinlock_init(borrow mut this.raw as *mut spinlock_t, this.name); }\n    }\n\n    /// Takes the lock with interrupts disabled; the guard restores them.\n    /// Safety: as SpinLock.lock.\n    public fn lock(borrow this) -> IrqSpinGuard<T> {\n        let state = unsafe { spin_lock_irqsave(this.cLock()) };\n        return IrqSpinGuard { lock: this, state: state };\n    }\n\n    fn cLock(borrow this) -> *mut spinlock_t => borrow this.raw as *spinlock_t as *mut spinlock_t;\n}\n\n@context(atomic)\npublic struct IrqSpinGuard<region r, T: Send> {\n    private lock: borrow(r) IrqSpinLock<T>;\n    private state: usize;\n\n    /// Safety: as SpinGuard.data.\n    public fn data(borrow mut this) -> borrow mut T =>\n        unsafe { Ptr.toBorrowMut(borrow this.lock.data as *T as *mut T) };\n\n    /// Safety: the guard holds the lock and the interrupt state spin_lock_irqsave returned.\n    drop {\n        unsafe { spin_unlock_irqrestore(this.lock.cLock(), this.state); }\n    }\n}\n\n/// A sleeping lock and the data it protects. Its C state points into itself once initialised, which is why it is\n/// made in place and never moved.\n@threadSafe\n@noncopy\npublic struct Mutex<T: Send> {\n    private raw: mutex;\n    private name: *CChar;\n    private data: T;\n\n    /// A mutex for a static (type-system.md §39): `static TABLE: Mutex<Table> = Mutex.constant(\"table\\0\".ptr as *CChar,\n    /// Table.empty());`. MUTEX_INIT_AT, given the mutex's own address, leave",
    23 =>
        "s it as `init` would (ADR 0027).\n    @constant\n    public fn constant(name: *CChar, data: T) -> Mutex<T> =>\n        Mutex { raw: MUTEX_INIT_AT(here, name), name: name, data: data };\n\n    /// Makes one in a KernelHeap box, initialised in place before this returns (type-system.md §33.3). `name` is its\n    /// lockdep class name: a static string ending in a zero byte.\n    public fn create(name: *CChar, data: T) -> Result<Box<Mutex<T>, KernelHeap>, AllocError> =>\n        Box.createIn(KernelHeap {}, Mutex { name: name, data: data });\n\n    /// Safety: as SpinLock.init.\n    @placed\n    fn init(borrow mut this) {\n        unsafe { mutex_init(borrow mut this.raw as *mut mutex, this.name); }\n    }\n\n    /// Takes the mutex, sleeping until it is free: forbidden in atomic contexts (its effect is `sleep`).\n    /// Safety: `raw` was initialised by `init`.\n    public fn lock(borrow this) -> MutexGuard<T> {\n        unsafe { mutex_lock(this.cLock()); }\n        return MutexGuard { lock: this };\n    }\n\n    fn cLock(borrow this) -> *mut mutex => borrow this.raw as *mutex as *mut mutex;\n}\n\n/// Holding the mutex. Not an atomic context: code under a mutex may sleep.\npublic struct MutexGuard<region r, T: Send> {\n    private lock: borrow(r) Mutex<T>;\n\n    /// Safety: as SpinGuard.data, for the mutex.\n    public fn data(borrow mut this) -> borrow mut T =>\n        unsafe { Ptr.toBorrowMut(borrow this.lock.data as *T as *mut T) };\n\n    /// Safety: the guard holds the mutex, which it took in `lock`.\n    drop {\n        unsafe { mutex_unlock(this.lock.cLock()); }\n    }\n}\n\n/// Preemption off on this CPU until dropped (CosmoOS's preempt_disable, nestable): an atomic context, so nothing\n/// under it may sleep. The thread stays on this CPU, but its interrupt handlers still run.\n@context(atomic)\n@noncopy\npublic struct PreemptGuard {\n    private unused: u8;\n\n    /// Safety: preempt_disable needs nothing; the guard's drop is its matching preempt_enable.\n    public fn enter() -> PreemptGuard {\n        unsafe { preempt_disable(); }\n        return PreemptGuard { unused: 0 };\n    }\n\n    /// Safety: the guard disabled preemption in `enter`, once.\n    drop {\n        unsafe { preempt_enable(); }\n    }\n}\n\n/// Interrupts off on this CPU until dropped (arch_irq_save, nestable): an atomic context, in which this CPU's thread\n/// cannot be preempted and its interrupt handlers cannot run.\n@context(atomic)\n@noncopy\npublic struct IrqGuard {\n    private state: usize;\n\n    /// Safety: arch_irq_save needs nothing and nests; the guard's drop restores the state it returned.\n    public fn enter() -> IrqGuard => IrqGuard { state: unsafe { arch_irq_save() } };\n\n    /// Safety: the state is the one arch_irq_save returned in `enter`, restored once.\n    drop {\n        unsafe { arch_irq_restore(this.state); }\n    }\n}\n\n/// The number of CPUs a PerCpu<T> holds a value for: CosmoOS's CONFIG_MAX_CPUS (kernel/include/kernel/percpu.h).\npublic const MAX_CPUS: usize = 64;\n\n/// One value of `T` per CPU (ADR 0024 §3.3, ADR 0025 §3.3). `p.local(borrow guard)` lends the calling CPU's value\n/// while interrupts are off, so neither another thread nor an interrupt handler on this CPU can reach it meanwhile, and\n/// no other CPU ever does. A second `local` of the same value on the same CPU, while the first is lent, panics.\n@threadSafe\n@noncopy\npublic struct PerCpu<T: Copy + Send> {\n    private values: [T; 64];\n    private lent: [bool; 64];\n\n    /// A per-CPU value for a static: `v` on every CPU.\n    @constant\n    public fn constant(v: T) -> PerCpu<T> => PerCpu { values: [v; 64], lent: [false; 64] };\n\n    /// This CPU's value, lent until the PerCpuRef drops, which also drops `guard` and so turns interrupts back on:\n    /// `TICKS.local(IrqGuard.enter())`.\n    /// Safety: with interrupts off on this CPU, the code that runs here until the guard drops is this thread's; the\n    /// value is this CPU's, which no other CPU's code reaches; `lent` makes a second loan on this CPU panic. So the\n    /// PerCpuRef this",
    _ =>
        " returns is the only way to the value while it lives.\n    public fn local(borrow this, guard: IrqGuard) -> PerCpuRef<T> {\n        let cpu = unsafe { arch_cpu_id() } as usize;\n        let lent = unsafe { Ptr.toBorrowMut(borrow this.lent[cpu] as *bool as *mut bool) };\n        if (lent) {\n            alreadyLent();\n        }\n        lent = true;\n        return PerCpuRef { owner: this, guard: guard, cpu: cpu };\n    }\n}\n\n/// A loan of one CPU's value of a PerCpu<T>, made by PerCpu.local; dropping it ends the loan.\n@noncopy\npublic struct PerCpuRef<region r, T: Copy + Send> {\n    private owner: borrow(r) PerCpu<T>;\n    private guard: IrqGuard;\n    private cpu: usize;\n\n    /// The value, for as long as this is borrowed.\n    /// Safety: as PerCpu.local: this loan is the only way to the value while it lives.\n    public fn get(borrow mut this) -> borrow mut T =>\n        unsafe { Ptr.toBorrowMut(borrow this.owner.values[this.cpu] as *T as *mut T) };\n\n    /// Safety: this loan set `lent` in PerCpu.local and is its only holder.\n    drop {\n        let lent = unsafe { Ptr.toBorrowMut(borrow this.owner.lent[this.cpu] as *bool as *mut bool) };\n        lent = false;\n    }\n}\n\n/// A second loan of one CPU's value of a PerCpu: a panic, through the runtime's ceesafe_panic (the kernel's), as an\n/// empty optional's unwrap is (type-system.md §22).\nfn alreadyLent() {\n    let none: u8? = null;\n    let unreachable = none!;\n}\n",
};

/// The whole text of file `k`.
fn libraryText(k: usize) -> Result<Vector<u8>, AllocError> {
    var text = Vector<u8>.create();
    for (j in 0..libraryChunkCount(k)) {
        appendText(mut text, libraryChunk(libraryFirstChunk(k) + j))?;
    }
    return Ok(text);
}
